The previous step established, via the Bayer-Hanck four-test combined Fisher statistic
$$\mathrm{EG\text{-}JOH\text{-}BO\text{-}BDM} = -2[\ln(P_{\mathrm{EG}}) + \ln(P_{\mathrm{JOH}}) + \ln(P_{\mathrm{BO}}) + \ln(P_{\mathrm{BDM}})],$$
that the five variables $\ln C_t$, $\ln E_t$, $\ln T_t$, $\ln M_t$, and $(\ln M_t)^2$ are cointegrated in the Ghana EKC model. By the Granger Representation Theorem, confirmed cointegration guarantees the existence of an error-correction representation.

Starting from the long-run cointegrating equation
$$\ln C_t = \alpha_0 + \alpha_1 \ln E_t + \alpha_2 \ln T_t + \alpha_3 \ln M_t + \alpha_4 (\ln M_t)^2 + u_t,$$
define the error-correction term
$$\mathrm{ECT}_{t-1} = \ln C_{t-1} - \alpha_0 - \alpha_1 \ln E_{t-1} - \alpha_2 \ln T_{t-1} - \alpha_3 \ln M_{t-1} - \alpha_4 (\ln M_{t-1})^2.$$

Derive the short-run ARDL-ECM equation for $\Delta \ln C_t$ by reparameterising the general ARDL$(p, q_1, q_2, q_3, q_4)$ levels equation into first-difference form. The final equation must include:
- an intercept $\beta_0$;
- short-run coefficients $\beta_{1i}$ on lagged $\Delta \ln C_{t-i}$ for $i = 1, \ldots, p-1$;
- short-run coefficients $\beta_{2i}$ on $\Delta \ln E_{t-i}$ for $i = 0, \ldots, q_1-1$;
- short-run coefficients $\beta_{3i}$ on $\Delta \ln T_{t-i}$ for $i = 0, \ldots, q_2-1$;
- short-run coefficients $\beta_{4i}$ on $\Delta \ln M_{t-i}$ for $i = 0, \ldots, q_3-1$;
- short-run coefficients $\beta_{5i}$ on $\Delta(\ln M_{t-i})^2$ for $i = 0, \ldots, q_4-1$, where $(\ln M_t)^2$ is treated as a single composite regressor;
- the speed-of-adjustment coefficient $\phi$ on $\mathrm{ECT}_{t-1}$;
- a white-noise error $\varepsilon_t$.

Write the complete ARDL-ECM equation in a single closed-form symbolic expression and state the sign restriction on $\phi$ required for convergence to the long-run equilibrium. The long-run coefficients $\alpha_0, \ldots, \alpha_4$ must appear only inside the definition of $\mathrm{ECT}_{t-1}$, not as separate regressors in the first-difference equation.

A metal-organic framework (MOF) is used in a pressure-swing adsorption (PSA) cycle. The deliverable (working) capacity from the previous step is

$$\Delta N = (\alpha S_{\mathrm{BET}} + \beta V_p) - A\exp\!\left(\frac{Q_{\mathrm{st}}}{RT}\right)P_{\mathrm{low}},$$

where:
- $\alpha, \beta > 0$ are positive constants relating uptake to BET surface area $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and pore volume $V_p$, respectively;
- $A > 0$ is the pre-exponential constant of the Henry constant $K_H = A\exp(Q_{\mathrm{st}}/(RT))$;
- $Q_{\mathrm{st}} > 0$ is the isosteric heat of adsorption (positive for exothermic adsorption);
- $R > 0$ is the gas constant, $T > 0$ is absolute temperature;
- $P_{\mathrm{low}} > 0$ is the fixed discharge pressure;
- All of $S_{\mathrm{BET}},\, V_p,\, \alpha,\, \beta,\, A,\, Q_{\mathrm{st}},\, R,\, P_{\mathrm{low}}$ are held fixed (independent of $T$).

**(i)** Compute $\dfrac{d(\Delta N)}{dT}$ as a closed-form symbolic expression in terms of the symbols listed above.

**(ii)** Determine the sign of $\dfrac{d(\Delta N)}{dT}$ for all $T > 0$ (using the sign conventions $Q_{\mathrm{st}} > 0$, $A > 0$, $P_{\mathrm{low}} > 0$), and state whether $\Delta N$ is strictly increasing, strictly decreasing, or non-monotone in $T$.

**(iii)** The PSA cycle is physically meaningful only when $\Delta N \geq 0$. Define $T^*$ as the unique temperature satisfying $\Delta N = 0$ exactly, i.e.,
$$A\exp\!\left(\frac{Q_{\mathrm{st}}}{RT^*}\right)P_{\mathrm{low}} = \alpha S_{\mathrm{BET}} + \beta V_p.$$
Derive a closed-form expression for $T^*$. Your answer must be expressed solely in terms of $Q_{\mathrm{st}},\, R,\, \alpha,\, S_{\mathrm{BET}},\, \beta,\, V_p,\, A,\, P_{\mathrm{low}}$; no new symbols may be introduced. Assume $\alpha S_{\mathrm{BET}} + \beta V_p > A\,P_{\mathrm{low}}$ so that $T^* > 0$.

In the DDRTree reverse graph embedding framework for single-cell trajectory inference, the full objective (with the soft-assignment matrix $Z$ held fixed) is
$$\mathcal{F}(Y_{\mathrm{mat}}) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{|V|} Z_{ij} \|x_i - y_j\|^2 + \lambda \cdot \mathrm{tr}\!\left(Y_{\mathrm{mat}}^\top L_W Y_{\mathrm{mat}}\right),$$
where:
- $X$ is the $n \times d$ data matrix (row $i$ is $x_i^\top \in \mathbb{R}^d$);
- $Y_{\mathrm{mat}}$ is the $|V| \times d$ matrix of principal-graph node positions (row $j$ is $y_j^\top \in \mathbb{R}^d$);
- $Z$ is the $n \times |V|$ non-negative soft-assignment matrix with $\sum_j Z_{ij} = 1$ for each $i$;
- $\Gamma = \mathrm{diag}\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} Z_{i1}, \ldots, \sum_{i=1}^{n} Z_{i|V|}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{|V|\times|V|}$ is the diagonal matrix with $\Gamma_{jj} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Z_{ij}$;
- $L_W \in \mathbb{R}^{|V|\times|V|}$ is the weighted graph Laplacian of the principal graph $W$ (symmetric positive-semidefinite), defined entry-wise as $(L_W)_{jk} = \sum_{\ell:(j,\ell)\in E(W)} w_{j\ell}$ if $j=k$; $-w_{jk}$ if $(j,k)\in E(W)$; $0$ otherwise;
- $\lambda > 0$ is a strictly positive scalar.

Derive the first-order stationarity equation for $Y_{\mathrm{mat}}$ obtained by setting the matrix gradient of $\mathcal{F}$ with respect to $Y_{\mathrm{mat}}$ equal to the $|V| \times d$ zero matrix $\mathbf{0}$, and express the result as a single closed-form matrix equation in $\boxed{\cdots}$, in terms of $\{\Gamma,\, \lambda,\, L_W,\, Y_{\mathrm{mat}},\, Z,\, X\}$.

