EU Regulation 1069/2009 governs animal by-products not intended for human consumption and establishes a three-tier risk classification: Category 1 (highest risk, including TSE-risk material), Category 2 (high risk, including disease-suspected material), and Category 3 (lowest risk tier). Catering waste (swill) — consumption-phase food waste from households, restaurants, hotels, and hospitals — is formally classified as a Category 3 animal by-product under this regulation, yet is explicitly prohibited as feed for farm animals other than fur animals.

The universe of microbial pathogen/hazard classes is 𝒫 = { viruses, spore-forming bacteria, mycotoxins/fungi, parasites }, with the following symbolic predicates defined for any H ∈ 𝒫:
- R_outbreak(H) ∈ {LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH}
- T_inactivation(H) : ordinal temperature threshold for reliable inactivation of H in a swill matrix, comparable to T_treatment via strict inequality >
- T_treatment : temperature achieved by standard heat-treatment of swill
- Z_potential(H) ∈ {TRUE, FALSE}
- Intra_recycle(H) ∈ {TRUE, FALSE}

Four criteria are defined: C₁(H) ≡ R_outbreak(H) = HIGH; C₂(H) ≡ T_inactivation(H) > T_treatment; C₃(H) ≡ Z_potential(H) = TRUE; C₄(H) ≡ Intra_recycle(H) = TRUE. Each criterion is independently sufficient for membership in J; membership requires at least one criterion to hold (classical Boolean OR).

Derive J, the set of microbial hazard classes in 𝒫 that provide sufficient scientific justification for the swill-feeding prohibition despite the Category 3 classification, and determine whether J = 𝒫.

Express your final answer as a single LaTeX \boxed{…} expression in terms of H, 𝒫, R_outbreak(H), T_inactivation(H), T_treatment, Z_potential(H), Intra_recycle(H), and J.

In a computer-assisted synthesis planning framework, the following symbols are defined:
- $S$ = total number of distinct reaction-pattern classes spanning the relevant chemical space (a fixed positive integer);
- $|R|$ = cardinality of the rule-based planner's encoded rule library ($0 \le |R| \le S$);
- $C$ = number of distinct reaction-pattern classes represented in the training dataset of the data-driven planner ($0 \le C \le S$);
- $\Delta_0$ = a fixed non-negative rational parameter satisfying $0 \le \Delta_0 \le 1 - |R|/S$.

A planner covers a reaction-pattern class if and only if at least one representative of that class appears in its rule library (rule-based) or training dataset (data-driven). The generalisation capacity of the data-driven planner is $G = C/S$ and that of the rule-based planner is $G_{\mathrm{rb}} = |R|/S$. The coverage gap is $\Delta = G - G_{\mathrm{rb}}$. Treat $|R|$ and $S$ as mutually independent symbolic parameters and $\Delta_0$ as a fixed constant held throughout all differentiations.

Assemble the $2\times 2$ Hessian matrix of the normalised minimum training-data fraction (the smallest value of $C/S$ such that $\Delta \ge \Delta_0$) with respect to the parameters $|R|$ and $S$, where row 1 and column 1 correspond to $|R|$ and row 2 and column 2 correspond to $S$. Express every entry in closed form; $\Delta_0$ must not appear in the final answer. State your answer as the matrix $H(f_{\min})$ in terms of $\{|R|, S\}$ only, in the form $\boxed{\cdots}$.

In the previous step, the partial order on the operator set {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**} under the structural subset relation ⊑ (defined by directed-arrow set inclusion) was established as a V-shape: ⊕ is the unique maximum, with ⊕* and ⊕** strictly below ⊕ and incomparable to each other. The directed-arrow sets are:

- directed_coupling_set(⊕) = {K_x → K_c, K_c → K_x}
- directed_coupling_set(⊕*) = {K_x → K_c}
- directed_coupling_set(⊕**) = {K_c → K_x}

The relation ⊑ is defined by: A ⊑ B if and only if directed_coupling_set(A) ⊆ directed_coupling_set(B) (set inclusion of directed-arrow sets). A poset (P, ⊑) is a **lattice** if and only if every pair of elements in P has both a meet (greatest lower bound, glb) and a join (least upper bound, lub) within P.

Using only the symbol set {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**} and directed-arrow set notation over the universe {K_x → K_c, K_c → K_x}, determine:

**(a)** For each of the three pairs — (⊕*, ⊕), (⊕**, ⊕), and (⊕*, ⊕**) — identify the meet and join within {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**} if they exist, or state that they do not exist within the set. Express each meet/join either as one of {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**} or as ∄ in {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**}.

**(b)** Does {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**} under ⊑ form a lattice? If not, identify the specific pair for which a meet or join fails to exist within the set, and express the missing bound symbolically as a directed-arrow set (a subset of {K_x → K_c, K_c → K_x}).

**(c)** State the lattice-theoretic conclusion as a single symbolic statement of the form: "{⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**} under ⊑ is [a lattice / not a lattice] because meet(⊕*, ⊕**) = [directed-arrow set expression] ∉ {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**}". Wrap this conclusion in \boxed{...}.

Your answer must address all three parts (a), (b), (c). The final boxed answer must be a single symbolic statement as described in part (c), using only the permitted symbols {⊕, ⊕*, ⊕**}, directed-arrow notation, and standard set-theoretic symbols (⊆, ∩, ∅, ∄, ∉).

In the GA–PNM coupled framework for bottom-up optimization of porous electrode microstructures in redox flow batteries, a genetic algorithm uses a scalar fitness function of the form
$$F = P_{\text{electrochem}} - \lambda \cdot P_{\text{pump}}$$
to guide evolutionary search, where $P_{\text{electrochem}}$ is the electrochemical power delivered by the electrode and $P_{\text{pump}}$ is the hydraulic pumping power consumed to drive electrolyte through the pore network.

The following symbols are defined:
- $A_s > 0$: total internal (active) surface area of the pore network;
- $K > 0$: effective hydraulic conductance (positive scalar);
- $Q > 0$: volumetric flow rate of electrolyte (fixed operating input, not a design variable);
- $\eta > 0$: lumped kinetic-transport efficiency prefactor (given positive constant for fixed chemistry and flow-field type);
- $\lambda > 0$: scalar penalty coefficient;
- $\alpha > 0$: geometry-dependent prefactor;
- $\beta < -1$: fixed scaling exponent.

A geometric constraint holds across the design space: $K = \alpha A_s^{\beta}$, where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are fixed parameters, not design variables.

Derive a closed-form symbolic expression for $\dfrac{dA_s^*}{d\lambda}$, the ordinary derivative of the GA-optimal active surface area $A_s^*$ with respect to the penalty coefficient $\lambda$, treating $\{\eta, Q, \alpha, \beta\}$ as fixed constants.

Express your final answer as a single closed-form expression in $\boxed{\cdots}$, written exclusively in terms of $\{\eta,\, \lambda,\, Q,\, \alpha,\, \beta\}$.

In the previous step, you established that the SP+/ETR+/TM+/XET-C chimera—bearing a canonical N-terminal signal peptide (SP), the ~45 aa extraterminal region (ETR), a transmembrane (TM) domain modeled after XTH33, and an intact XET-C domain—is predicted to exhibit the phenotype triple (Φ_dest = plasma membrane, Φ_BFA = sensitive, Φ_EXPO = lost), because SP-mediated conventional protein secretion (CPS) dominates and the TM domain anchors the protein at the plasma membrane.

Now consider a new structural variant, SP−/ETR+/TM+/XET-C, generated by removing the N-terminal SP from that chimera while retaining the ETR, TM domain, and XET-C domain unchanged.

Using the secretion-pathway rules established in this framework—specifically:
(i) CPS requires an N-terminal SP for ER entry and is BFA-sensitive; absence of SP renders CPS completely inaccessible;
(ii) UPS via EXPOs (marked by Exo70E2-GFP colocalization, BFA-insensitive) requires ETR as the positive instructive signal for EXPO recruitment and operates exclusively on soluble, cytosolic leaderless (SP−, TM−) cargo;
(iii) A TM domain requires CPS/translocon machinery for ER membrane insertion; without SP, the TM segment is exposed in the cytosol, causing misfolding or aggregation and precluding productive EXPO loading;

derive the predicted phenotype triple (Φ_dest, Φ_BFA, Φ_EXPO) for the SP−/ETR+/TM+/XET-C chimera.

Your derivation must apply each rule sequentially to each structural feature and arrive at a uniquely determined triple. Express your final answer using only the allowed outcome labels specified in the World Contract, wrapped in \boxed{...}.
